WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:06.180
Some of the best TV series of all time were
canceled too soon, but those overlooked classics

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.559
at least had enough episodes air to build
up a fanbase that was devastated when the

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.440
shows were pulled before their time.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.760
Imagine how it must feel to be a creator,
producer, or star of a TV series that performed

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.100
so poorly that it was ripped off the air after
only a single episode.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.100
Here are some shows that share that very unfortunate
distinction.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:27.840
Co-Ed Fever

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:34.120
The biggest movie comedy of 1978 was National
Lampoon's Animal House, so naturally all three

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.540
broadcast TV networks at the time looked to
cash in on the craze.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:43.410
CBS's attempt, Co-Ed Fever, was canceled before
it even debuted in its regular time slot,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.720
thwarting countless wacky hijinks ...

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.840
"Why don't we all go over to Dean McPhee's
house and glue his wife to the ceiling?"

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.640
After the TV premiere of Rocky in 1979, CBS
had some time to fill, and aired a "special

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.940
preview" of the show, which was set on the
campus of a former women's college that had

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.000
just started admitting men.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.080
While the show ranked a reasonable 19th in
the Nielsen ratings for the week, it lost

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.920
so much of the Rocky audience that CBS shut
it down completely.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.760
Public Morals

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.720
In the '80s and '90s, producer Steven Bochco,
the creator of critically acclaimed hit shows

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.300
such as NYPD Blue, Hill Street Blues, and
L.A. Law, was just about the king of TV.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:26.380
CBS jumped at the chance to get into the Bochco
business, and committed the laugh track-laden

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.300
Public Morals to its fall 1996 schedule.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.840
Ratings and reviews for its October debut
were dismal, and the show about a vice squad

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.310
also suffered from bad timing: it premiered
in an election year that included a debate

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.800
over whether TV was growing too vulgar…because
of shows like Public Morals.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.080
CBS canceled it after just one controversial
airing.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.340
Emily's Reasons Why Not

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.540
ABC reportedly spent $5 million on an ad campaign
for the midseason launch of Emily's Reasons

00:01:54.549 --> 00:02:00.270
Why Not, a 2006 sitcom about how dating is
hard and weird, starring the always likable

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.060
Heather Graham.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.120
Graham starred as Emily, a woman unlucky in
love trying to figure out who not to date

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:06.620
and why.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.560
Viewers must have decided there were plenty
of reasons why not to give the show a chance:

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:15.950
it lost more than a third of the audience
of its lead-in and lost a quarter of the audience

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.980
of The Bachelorette, the show that had occupied
its time slot a year earlier, which led to

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.280
ABC immediately pulling the plug.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.740
Secret Talents of the Stars

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.560
One part Circus of the Stars, one part American
Idol, this reality competition show featured

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:36.140
celebrities known for one talent demonstrating
a different talent the general public didn't

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.040
know they had.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.459
Just over 4-point-5 million people tuned in
to watch, to mention just one representative

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.790
example, country singer Clint Black try stand-up
comedy ...

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.840
"I am gonna use index cards tonight."

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.560
Viewers at home were supposed to vote for
their favorites each week—had CBS not canceled

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.740
the show after its first episode in April
2008.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:58.080
Quarterlife

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.060
High-quality TV streaming on the internet?

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.960
It's common today, but in 2008 it was revolutionary.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:10.040
Marshall Herskovitz and Edward Zwick, creators
of critically acclaimed dramas thirtysomething

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:16.190
and My So-Called Life, debuted Quarterlife
on MySpace, which was a hot social media platform

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:16.800
at the time.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.840
The show was made to reflect its medium—episodes
were short and resembled YouTube clips, and

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.680
it looked like it was shot on webcams or hidden
cameras.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:29.440
While there was no real precedent for what
constituted a hit streaming series in 2008,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.160
enough people watched that NBC decided to
put it on terrestrial television.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.600
After taking a few episodes and pasting them
together to fit an hour-long programming block,

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:43.300
its premiere brought in just 3.1 million viewers—the
worst mid-season ratings of an NBC show in

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.820
nearly two decades.

00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:48.580
The network canceled the show and dumped the
five remaining prepared episodes onto its

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.060
cable counterpart, Bravo.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.220
South of Sunset

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:58.010
Eagles singer and guitarist Glenn Frey didn't
fare nearly as well in his foray into dramatic

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.000
television as he'd done with smooth California
rock.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.420
Very few viewers got a peaceful easy feeling
with South of Sunset, a lightly comic detective

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:11.500
show in which Frey starred as a former movie
studio security chief who opens his own private

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.180
investigations company in Beverly Hills.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.300
Despite CBS frequently airing ads for the
show during its broadcast of the 1993 World

00:04:18.309 --> 00:04:23.089
Series just before the premiere episode, only
nine percent of viewers tuned their dials

00:04:23.089 --> 00:04:24.180
to South of Sunset.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.560
The Los Angeles Times reported that the show
amassed "the lowest premiere ratings on record"

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.540
for a show on one of the Big Three networks
to date.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.940
CBS put the show "on hiatus" almost immediately,
but South of Sunset never returned to the

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:37.440
schedule.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.720
Osbournes: Reloaded

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.419
An attempted revival of MTV's The Osbournes,
Osbournes: Reloaded debuted in a plum post-American

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.480
Idol time slot in 2009.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:53.340
It presented metal god Ozzy Osbourne and his
family at the center of a variety show featuring

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:58.560
pre-taped segments, sketches, audience participation
bits, and musical performances.

00:04:58.689 --> 00:05:03.200
Following a high-profile preview episode,
nearly one in five Fox affiliates refused

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:08.249
to air further episodes of Osbournes: Reloaded
due to concerns over racy material and bad

00:05:08.249 --> 00:05:08.760
language.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:14.480
Critics were brutal: the Washington Post called
it "a revolting debacle" and "must-flee TV.",

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.260
Fox didn't even bother to air the rest of
the episodes already in the can.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:20.200
Lawless

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.500
Fox apparently didn't learn much from CBS's
disastrous South of Sunset, and ordered up

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.460
another series about a private investigator
starring a famous non-actor.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:34.580
Lawless starred standout University of Oklahoma
linebacker and Seattle Seahawks washout Brian

00:05:34.599 --> 00:05:40.020
"The Boz" Bosworth as an ex-Special Forces
tough guy named John Lawless who unravels

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.500
mysteries in Miami.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:47.240
Very few people wanted to see "The Boz" off
the gridiron, and Fox canceled the 1997 series

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.650
after its premiere episode.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.780
"This just doesn't seem to be your lucky day."

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.080
Thanks for watching!

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.780
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.280
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

